an><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">X ATHLETICS 5</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">X AWARENESS 3 (avoid ambush)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">DODGE </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">LARCENY </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">STEALTH </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">BUREAUCRACY </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">LINGUISTICS 1</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">X RIDE 3</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">SAIL </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">SOCIALIZE </span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">WILLPOWER 5</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">ESSENCE 1</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">PERSONAL 13</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">PERIPHERAL 33</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Committed 8</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Weapon acc dmg parry over tags</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Longsword 13 13 7 2 lethal melee balanced</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Shield 13 11 8 1 bashing shield melee</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">( note house rule that shield adds 1 to parry value and dv would be 1 less if only wielding one of the weapons)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">CHARMS 15</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Dipping swallow defense 2m reflexive instant</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">bulwark stance 5m reflexive till next turn</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">monkey leaping technique 2m supplemental instant</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">soaring crane leap 3m reflexive instant </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">sensory acuity prana 5m reflexive scene</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">graceful crane stance 3m reflexive scene</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">War lion stance 2m reflexive scene</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">SOLAR COUNTERATTACK 3M REFLEXIVE INSTANT </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">FIVEFOLD BULWARK STANCE 5M 1WP SIMPLE SCENE </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">HEAVENLY GUARDINA DEFENSE 1I/SUC +4M OR 4M OR 4M 1WP INSTANT REFLEXIVE</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">READY IN EIGHT DIRECTIONS STANCE 5M SIMPLE TILL NEXT ACTION</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">EXCELLENT STRIKE 3M SUPPLEMENTAL INSTANT</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">ONE WEAPON TWO BLOWS 3M REFLEXIVE INSTANT</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">FLASHING EDGE OF DAWN 4M 1WP REFLEXIVE INSTANT</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">CALM AND READY FOCUS - PERMANENT</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">PEONY BLOSSOM ATTACK <br /> PERFECT STRIKE DISCIPLINE <br /> Iron Whirlwind attack <br /> Seasoned beast rider approach</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">MERITS </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Familiar (child of simhata god Haki) 3</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Quick draw 4</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Armor of the indomitable spirit </span></span>4</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Languages 1 (forest tongue)</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Armor of the indomitable spirit Art 4 heavy armor</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">when attuning may commit 3 extra motes to don your convictions as long as you are working towards a intimacy add the bonus resolve to the armors soak if done this causes anyone who looks upon the armor to automatically succeed at a read intent action for whatever intimacy is being channeled into it<br /> <br /> Evocations (Note these were unlocked for free with the assistance of the parties Zenith Lore supernal)<br /> <br /> </span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">essence 1</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">once per scene reuse a defining or major intimacy to strengthen resolve, it must be the one represented on the armor and this use is obvious as it flares the sigils that inscribe themselves across it</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">essence 1</span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">boost resolve by essence as non charm dice<br /> (these never came up and never got finished so they could honestly be replaced by the environmental sealing and part of the free power)<br /> </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Haki</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The simhata is a proud and majestic creature, combining the best aspects of lion and horse in its build, with vicious tempers and a predatory gaze. They roam Southern savannas and Eastern grasslands, disemboweling prey with powerful claws before moving in for the killing bite. While they are fearsome predators, they have an uneasy relationship with nearby humans\u2014though a simhata is by no means averse to preying on human flesh, they can also be tamed to serve as ferociously loyal mounts. The affinity between human and simhata goes back to their origins in distant prehistory as the war-mounts of the Exalted, and even in the Time of Tumult, the lion-horse seems to have an inborn desire to serve any of the Chosen who prove themselves worthy of it.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Essence: 1; Willpower: 6; Join Battle: 6 dice</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Health Levels: -0x3/-1x3/-2x3/-4/Incap. A simhata will flee after taking 7+ levels of damage,</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">but will never abandon a bonded rider, even if it must sacrifice its own life to save his.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Speed Bonus: +5 (increased speed for divine heritage) </span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Actions: Feats of Strength: 9 dice (may attempt Strength 5 feats); Intimidate: 10 dice; Senses: 5 dice (see Keen Nose); Tracking: 4 dice (see Keen Nose)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Resolve 4, Guile 1</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Combat</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Attack (Bite): 8 dice (Damage 17)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Attack (Claws): 10 dice (Damage 14)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Attack (Grapple): 7 dice (8 dice to control)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Combat Movement: 13 dice</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Evasion 5, Parry 3</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Soak/Hardness: 11/5 (hide acts as light artifact armor to represent his divine heritage)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Special Attacks</i></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Brutal Kick: Smaller enemies that take 5+ damage from the simhata\u2019s withering claw attacks</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">are knocked back one range band and fall prone.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Savaging Maw: Any decisive bite attack that the simhata makes against an enemy who is prone or in Initiative Crash adds extra successes to the damage roll.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Swift Advance: Whenever the simhata moves to keep pace with an enemy it has successfully</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">rushed, it may pay a point of Willpower to reflexively make a decisive attack against that enemy.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Raging Lion-Horse Fury: Whenever damage fills one of the simhata\u2019s -2 or lower health levels, it enters a state of frenzy. A simhata who witnesses its bonded rider being killed (see below) also enters this berserker state. It ignores wound penalties and gains a point of Willpower on each turn, which must be spent before its next turn to add a success to a combat-related action or to resist influence that would cause it to cease fighting or impede its ability to fight. An enraged simhata will not flee from even the most desperately impossible battles. However, a rider who has bonded to the simhata may attempt to calm its rage with a (Charisma + Ride) roll at difficulty 4, which cannot be placed in a flurry.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Foe-Maiming Guardian\u2019s Fangs (Magical, 1m): Whenever the simhata makes a decisive bite</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">attack against an enemy who has attacked or threatened its bonded rider, the Exalt may pay one mote to apply the double 10s rule to the damage roll. If he is currently suffering any wound penalties due to a confrontation involving that enemy, then the amount of his wound penalties are</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">added as bonus dice to the hound\u2019s attack roll.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Merits</i></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Keen Nose: Simhata double 9s on scent-based Perception rolls.<br /> Swift hooved: Apply double 9's to disengage, withdraw, or rush attacks (added to represent his godblood nature)</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Predator\u2019s Hunger: Whenever a simhata rolls Join Battle against an enemy it could conceivably eat, if it has not eaten within the last day or more, it may reroll any 1s until they fail to appear. A simhata\u2019s master can train it to apply this Merit to all Join Battle rolls as a latent ability by conditioning it to associate battle with being given free rein to devour his enemies.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Bonding (Latent): A wild simhata that is successfully tamed\u2014a task that involves a great deal of hard effort and raw meat\u2014bonds to its rider, forming a Major Tie of loyalty to them. While it</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">may not be well-tempered or easy to handle, its devotion is unwavering, it will accept no other</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">rider, and anyone foolish enough to attack its master will answer to its claws. However, the</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">simhata were bred in the ancient past as war-steeds of the Exalted. Even a bonded simhata will allow an Exalt to ride it if his anima is flaring, and when bonded to an Exalt, the Tie they form is Defining and overrides any existing bonds to un-Exalted riders.</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Cloud-Crossing Leap (Latent): A simhata that has been trained to jump can achieve incredible</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">heights. As long as it has two effective range bands of movement prior to a jump to build up</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">speed, it may pay a point of Willpower to leap a distance comparable to what a Solar can achieve</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#263238\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">with Monkey Leap Technique (p. XX).</span></span><br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1166-korekore","name":"korekore","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1166_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-23T12:58:25-07:00","id":872601,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sanctaphrax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/835958-ex3-how-to-challenge-a-full-solar-circle?p=869047#post869047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Huh.<br /> <br /> I don't know why but I feel kinda sad about that.<br /> <br /> If the sheer crunch and complexity of EX3 is driving you away, despite your obvious willingness to put in time, that's not a good sign for the game's future.<br /> <br /> Well, good luck with whatever you play next.</div> </div> </div> </div>The system is <i>hard</i> for Storytellers, I think<br /> <br /> There's so much going on, you need to take all your players' individual charm selections into very careful consideration, and the NPC stats essentially require you to sit down and spend time memorising and creating tactical plans for the player's actions before the combat our you'll find that half way through trying to juggle the charms of five immaculates and their individual combat status (willpower, wounds, various mote types etc) your ability to actually make combat fun and exciting for the players degrades.<br /> <br /> It's a great game if you are willing to do that. If you aren't, then it quickly approaches being un-runnable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/117-solar","name":"Solar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar117_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1267"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-23T17:08:10-07:00","id":872742,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Segev</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/835958-ex3-how-to-challenge-a-full-solar-circle?p=871609#post871609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm glad it's helpful to some. I will say that I actually think this kind of encounter design leads to better games, overall, for many of the reasons you noted, kindledRadial. I think too often GMs get into a habit of viewing combat as a &quot;kill or be killed&quot; thing, &quot;to the death,&quot; and that leads to a trap wherein one might accidentally start equating &quot;having a fight to the death&quot; with &quot;a challenge.&quot; Really, that isn't the challenge, even if it is the source of difficulty and risk. The challenge should always be something with greater stakes to the player than merely &quot;will my character survive?&quot;<br /> <br /> I know, life-or-death is one of the greatest true risks out there, but because this is a game, we often trivialize it. It also means, with stakes at the &quot;do or die&quot; level, that the PCs <u>must</u> always win. If they don't, they die, and that means that the game is over, if only for that character. You can have victory become a more meaningfully uncertain quality of a conflict if there is something at stake that, even if lost, does not stop the PCs from being able to continue participating in the game's ongoing events.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're absolutely right, and I'd like to point out that there is this really great mechanical system in Ex3 to model this: withering damage. Withering damage can be anything, so there's nothing stopping you really from narrating a non-combat loss whenever someone loses initiative, like saying that the five initiative damage your Dawn just took came from this bandit feinting forward, then ducking right and swiping a bag of rubies.<br /> <br /> But you can only use this system to apply pressure to your PCs if they <i>take</i> any withering damage. When your PCs consistently roll well enough to flatten their enemies on round one or two, it doesn't really matter what those initiative points represent. And if you model this the normal way (the bandit takes a miscellaneous action to lift the rubies) then either you forgo the right to deal combat damage or you put yourself at deeper penalties by flurrying.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure exactly how you model this or that challenge at your table, but every meaningful challenge <i>should</i> involve rolls unless it's social. Otherwise, your party will resent you for these losses you gave them that they were powerless to stop. So then you have to model that challenge somehow in the system, and whether you are using combat, simple or opposed rolls, naval or military strategies, or the crafting system, I've yet to find anything in the core book which a starting party can't totally wreck at the outset.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Segev</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/835958-ex3-how-to-challenge-a-full-solar-circle?p=871609#post871609\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you really need the &quot;fight is challenging just because the stats are that grueling&quot; thing...save it for when you've got a chance to do one-on-ones or other things that will allow you to separate the PCs into groups that fight at more even levels, and pit them against challenges for them. It will likely be rare, so you can do it this way without always splitting up the party every time conflict breaks out.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would sincerely encourage you to do this more often. As I've mentioned down-thread a ways, the rewards for specialization in Exalted are so great that I generally find it's impossible to challenge everyone at once with the same thing; a fun fight for the Dawn will most likely murder the rest of your party, and what is fun for them will leave your Dawn wondering why they invested in 5+ combat charms.<br /> <br /> I like the &quot;boss and his lieutenants&quot; approach, where one of the enemies is the clearly labeled badass of the enemy party that I make for my Dawn, while his lieutenants engage in the sort of mission-objective shenanigans you suggested in ways that engage everyone else. So maybe a gang of bandits attack the caravan, led by a Lunar Full-Moon, but while the Dawn engages that guy the Twilight has to handle the de-materialized Agata the bandits summoned as get-away drivers, the Zenith compels the mortal caravan guards to action and rolls opposed War with the Lunar's savvy strategist, the Night is the only person who notices a saboteur aboard the main train in time to stop them, and the Eclipse puts some of those Ride skills to use chasing down any bandits that try to escape with loot on horseback.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","name":"kindledRadial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1318_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-25T07:19:26-07:00","id":873739,"title":"","content":"I would hesitate to make withering damage have &quot;real&quot; consequences, in and of itself. It's meant to be the back-and-forth of attacks that don't &quot;do&quot; anything, but make whoever just hit harder come off as more &quot;in control&quot; of the fight. It's a very cinematic thing. Swiping something really should be a Gambit (which deals with decisive attacks), and IS a good way to have non-lethal decisive attack actions.<br /> <br /> The advice about non-kill objectives is more meta than the mechanics, honestly. It's about using the mechanics as tools rather than about adapting them to represent something new. Which...probably isn't clear, but what I'm trying to get at is that you don't need to refluff withering attacks for this. Use the movement rules, the choice of targets of attacks, and the chance to take non-attack actions. The bandit raiders move away from the heroes and towards the &quot;stuff,&quot; and spend actions taking stuff and fleeing rather than trying to fight...if the PCs let them (or, rather, fail to stop them). The assassins choose to target the squishy mortal targets, and are trying to avoid the tanky dangerous Exalted bodyguards. That kind of thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/827-segev","name":"Segev","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1001"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-25T11:20:23-07:00","id":873887,"title":"","content":"Well I think both interpretations are valid, depending on how much narrative weight you give the loot in this scene. If the heroes are very much dedicated to defending it as their main objective, then I think tying it to their initiative (perhaps with each one guarding a different wagon) makes sense and is fun. If it's a secondary objective such that they &quot;lose&quot; whether they are crashed or the loot is swiped, maybe roll the loot itself into combat with its own depletable initiative pool. Then allow players to transfer initiative into it as a misc action, for a fun tug of war mechanic.<br /> <br /> Anyway, setting the vagaries of this particular example aside, my point is that withering damage can model many many things whereas decisive damage always models physical damage. You can also do these secondary objectives as gambits (effectively trading initiative from combat for a non-combat advantage) but that means you <i>still </i>need to be fighting the very tough PC exalts in order to gain the points to out fox them, which as I have said is a very very tall order for anything currently statted in this system. If you want those secondary objectives to be more challenging than standard combat, then I recommend using a system that allows enemies to succeed without getting into opposed rolls with the Solar exalts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","name":"kindledRadial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1318_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-25T11:54:30-07:00","id":873918,"title":"","content":"One thing you can do to challenge powerful PCs without trying to beat them down with ever-larger hammers: force them to make choices about where they are. When the enemies queue up in a conga-line to get their asses beat it's easy to feel like the players are invincible. And you are right that issuing ST-fiat without a PC having the opportunity to contest a loss is likely to lead to resentment. But if you instead present the players with the sadistic choice of &quot;Your enemies are taking the following actions in multiple locations; where do you want to stop them?&quot; they can't really complain about the ST not fighting fair. Instead of the challenge being &quot;How strong is your sword arm?&quot; it becomes &quot;How many fires can you put out at once?&quot;<br /> <br /> This is the kind of evolution that a Solars game ought to naturally take. When the stakes are small and local, it's easy for the PCs to overwhelm any opposition through personal force of arms. But as they become invested in larger concerns, the scope ought to grow as well. The simple fact that the PCs cannot be everywhere at once is something to consider. If they can win any single battle, great - but what about when there are three battles to fight at the same time? Now the problem is not <i>whether </i>they win, but <i>where</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Share your wonders in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/823884-3e-the-artifact-and-evocation-workshop#post823884\" target=\"_blank\">The Artifact and Evocation Workshop</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1315-ferryman","name":"Ferryman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1315_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"960"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-25T15:21:39-07:00","id":874059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ferryman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/835958-ex3-how-to-challenge-a-full-solar-circle?p=873918#post873918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing you can do to challenge powerful PCs without trying to beat them down with ever-larger hammers: force them to make choices about where they are. When the enemies queue up in a conga-line to get their asses beat it's easy to feel like the players are invincible. And you are right that issuing ST-fiat without a PC having the opportunity to contest a loss is likely to lead to resentment. But if you instead present the players with the sadistic choice of &quot;Your enemies are taking the following actions in multiple locations; where do you want to stop them?&quot; they can't really complain about the ST not fighting fair. Instead of the challenge being &quot;How strong is your sword arm?&quot; it becomes &quot;How many fires can you put out at once?&quot;<br /> <br /> This is the kind of evolution that a Solars game ought to naturally take. When the stakes are small and local, it's easy for the PCs to overwhelm any opposition through personal force of arms. But as they become invested in larger concerns, the scope ought to grow as well. The simple fact that the PCs cannot be everywhere at once is something to consider. If they can win any single battle, great - but what about when there are three battles to fight at the same time? Now the problem is not <i>whether </i>they win, but <i>where</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is really solid advice, especially if you are going for a stationary story.<br /> <br /> In a mobile game where the party travels more or less alone, this sort of story will force hard choices to be made and very rarely will every objective be completed. That can be frustrating for players after a while if you don't take breaks from it. But in a stationary game about city-building or what not, you have the added challenge of administration. Your Dawn caste can't be everywhere at once to stop a hostile invasion, but maybe if they've done a good job training the town guard and they pick their battles well they might be able to save everyone after all. If your players enjoy world-building and don't mind a game that deals less with foreign locales and secret treasures, this can be great fun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1318-kindledradial","name":"kindledRadial","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1318_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-25T19:30:51-07:00","id":874193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ferryman</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/solar-exalted/835958-ex3-how-to-challenge-a-full-solar-circle?p=873918#post873918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing you can do to challenge powerful PCs without trying to beat them down with ever-larger hammers: force them to make choices about where they are. When the enemies queue up in a conga-line to get their asses beat it's easy to feel like the players are invincible. And you are right that issuing ST-fiat without a PC having the opportunity to contest a loss is likely to lead to resentment. But if you instead present the players with the sadistic choice of &quot;Your enemies are taking the following actions in multiple locations; where do you want to stop them?&quot; they can't really complain about the ST not fighting fair. Instead of the challenge being &quot;How strong is your sword arm?&quot; it becomes &quot;How many fires can you put out at once?&quot;<br /> <br /> This is the kind of evolution that a Solars game ought to naturally take. When the stakes are small and local, it's easy for the PCs to overwhelm any opposition through personal force of arms. But as they become invested in larger concerns, the scope ought to grow as well. The simple fact that the PCs cannot be everywhere at once is something to consider. If they can win any single battle, great - but what about when there are three battles to fight at the same time? Now the problem is not <i>whether </i>they win, but <i>where</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Amen! This dynamic is why my players have found their game get <i>harder</i> over time rather than easier, despite the fact that each of their individual enemies haven't gotten especially stronger while they have themselves grown enormously in personal power.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10291-zeesun","name":"Zeesun","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"252"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}